            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Four Seasons</strong><br>\nVivaldi's most famous work, \"The Four Seasons,\" is a set of four violin concertos. Each concerto is associated with a season of the year, and they were published in 1725 as part of a set of twelve concertos titled \"Il cimento dell'armonia e dell'inventione\" (The Contest Between Harmony and Invention). \"The Four Seasons\" is known for its vibrant melodies and the imaginative depiction of scenes from each season.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Opera Compositions</strong><br>\nBesides instrumental music, Vivaldi was also a prolific opera composer, with more than 40 operas to his name. He began producing operas in the early 18th century and enjoyed success as an opera impresario. He worked in several cities, including Venice, Rome, and Vienna, bringing his operas to different audiences across Europe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Style and Influence</strong><br>\nVivaldi's music style is characterized by clarity, brightness, and fast-moving notes that convey emotion and technical skill. He was one of the composers who established the fast-slow-fast movement structure of concertos. His work influenced many composers, including Johann Sebastian Bach, who transcribed several of his concertos for other instruments.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
