          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Travels and Late Career</strong><br>\nIn the 1720s and 1730s, Vivaldi traveled to various European cities to promote his operas. Despite his initial popularity, his career began to decline, and he faced financial difficulties. In 1740, Vivaldi moved to Vienna, hoping to reclaim his success, but his efforts were short-lived.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Rediscovery</strong><br>\nAntonio Vivaldi died on July 28, 1741, in poverty in Vienna and was buried in a simple grave. After his death, his music was largely forgotten until the 20th century when a resurgence of interest in baroque music led to the rediscovery of his work. Today, he is celebrated as one of the greatest baroque composers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vivaldi's Legacy</strong><br>\nVivaldi's influence extends beyond the baroque period, with his music continuing to be performed regularly. His contributions to the development of the concerto grosso and solo concerto form are particularly noteworthy. His vivid compositions have made him an integral part of classical music education and appreciation around the world.</p>\n"
              },
